      "seed": 205716395,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 117784069,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790080806221,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "Syntax        Meaning\n{{ ... }}        Display a value\n{% ... %}        Execute template logic\n{# ... #}        Comment",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Syntax        Meaning\n{{ ... }}        Display a value\n{% ... %}        Execute template logic\n{# ... #}        Comment",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
      "id": "RQ7-MfxIkrr8EACwcrGmU",
      "type": "text",
      "x": 364.3620551227009,
      "y": 1270.5360588348337,
      "width": 1833.0113525390625,
      "height": 3555,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 2,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "index": "a8",
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1903078181,
      "version": 4,
      "versionNonce": 611784747,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1790084777429,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. If the URL changes, you don't have to change every template\n\nSuppose later you change:\n\n@app.get(\"/about\")\n\nto:\n\n@app.get(\"/about-us\")\n\nWith manual URLs, you'd have to find every:\n\n<a href=\"/about\">\n\nand change it.\n\nWith url_for():\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('about') }}\">About</a>\n\nyou don't change the template. FastAPI/Starlette generates the new URL from the route.\n\n2. It becomes even more useful with dynamic URLs\n\nImagine:\n\n@app.get(\"/users/{user_id}\", name=\"user\")\ndef user(user_id: int):\n    ...\n\nManually:\n\n<a href=\"/users/{{ user.id }}\">Profile</a>\n\nUsing url_for():\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('user', user_id=user.id) }}\">\n    Profile\n</a>\n\nYou're saying:\n\n\"Give me the URL for the user route, with this user_id.\"\n\n3. Think of it as a connection between your route and template\n\nInstead of:\n\nTemplate\n   ↓\n\"/about\"  ← hardcoded string\n\nyou have:\n\nTemplate\n   ↓\nurl_for(\"about\")\n   ↓\nFind the route named \"about\"\n   ↓\n\"/about\"\n\nSo manual URL = hardcoded, while url_for() = route-aware.\n\nOne important point\n\nFor a tiny project, this:\n\n<a href=\"/about\">About</a>\n\nis completely fine.\n\nBut as your FastAPI project grows, you'll have many routes and templates. Using:\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('about') }}\">About</a>\n\nmakes your application easier to modify and maintain.\n\nSo don't think \"url_for() is required.\" Think \"it's a safer and more maintainable way to reference routes.\"",
      "fontSize": 36,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. If the URL changes, you don't have to change every template\n\nSuppose later you change:\n\n@app.get(\"/about\")\n\nto:\n\n@app.get(\"/about-us\")\n\nWith manual URLs, you'd have to find every:\n\n<a href=\"/about\">\n\nand change it.\n\nWith url_for():\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('about') }}\">About</a>\n\nyou don't change the template. FastAPI/Starlette generates the new URL from the route.\n\n2. It becomes even more useful with dynamic URLs\n\nImagine:\n\n@app.get(\"/users/{user_id}\", name=\"user\")\ndef user(user_id: int):\n    ...\n\nManually:\n\n<a href=\"/users/{{ user.id }}\">Profile</a>\n\nUsing url_for():\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('user', user_id=user.id) }}\">\n    Profile\n</a>\n\nYou're saying:\n\n\"Give me the URL for the user route, with this user_id.\"\n\n3. Think of it as a connection between your route and template\n\nInstead of:\n\nTemplate\n   ↓\n\"/about\"  ← hardcoded string\n\nyou have:\n\nTemplate\n   ↓\nurl_for(\"about\")\n   ↓\nFind the route named \"about\"\n   ↓\n\"/about\"\n\nSo manual URL = hardcoded, while url_for() = route-aware.\n\nOne important point\n\nFor a tiny project, this:\n\n<a href=\"/about\">About</a>\n\nis completely fine.\n\nBut as your FastAPI project grows, you'll have many routes and templates. Using:\n\n<a href=\"{{ url_for('about') }}\">About</a>\n\nmakes your application easier to modify and maintain.\n\nSo don't think \"url_for() is required.\" Think \"it's a safer and more maintainable way to reference routes.\"",
      "autoResize": true,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    }
  ],
